What is Queue? ● Queue is a linear data structure that follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle. ● Data is inserted from the rear end and deleted from the front end. ● Example: A line of people waiting for a ticket.
How is Queue represented? ● A queue can be represented using an array. ● It can also be represented using a linked list. ● Two pointers, front and rear, are generally used to perform queue operations.
Enlist types of Queues. The main types of queues are:
Linear Queue
Circular Queue
Priority Queue
Deque (Double-Ended Queue)
What is the concept of Circular Queue? ● A circular queue is a queue in which the last position is connected back to the first position. ● When the rear reaches the last position, it can move back to the first position if space is available. ● It helps in efficiently utilizing the empty spaces in a queue.
Applications of Queue ● Queues are used in CPU scheduling and printer scheduling. ● They are used in handling requests and managing data in the order of arrival. ● They are also used in simulation of real-life waiting lines.
What is the Priority Queue? ● A priority queue is a collection of elements in which each element is assigned a priority. ● An element with higher priority is processed before an element with lower priority. ● If two elements have the same priority, they are processed according to their order of insertion.
What is Deque? ● Deque stands for Double-Ended Queue. ● It is a linear data structure in which elements can be inserted and deleted from both front and rear ends. ● Elements cannot be inserted or deleted from the middle.
